      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2016 Elsevier Ireland Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "The performance of contemporary second-generation drug-eluting stents (DESs) for percutaneous chronic total occlusion (CTO) intervention is not well established. The present study compared the efficacy and safety outcomes of second-generation DESs with those of first-generation DESs in CTO-percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "This retrospective analysis included 1049 consecutive CTO patients who underwent successful DES implantation (first-generation; 487 vs. second-generation; 562 patients) between March 2003 and August 2014. The primary endpoint was the composite of all-cause death, Q-wave myocardial infarction (MI), or target-vessel revascularization (TVR)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "During a follow-up of 3 years, the primary endpoint incidence was 10.1% for second- and 7.7% for first-generation DES (p=0.30). After multivariable adjustment, there was no significant difference between these groups in terms of the risk of composite of death, Q-wave MI, or TVR (Hazard ratio [HR] 1.42, 95% confidence interval [CI] 0.88-2.28, p=0.15) nor in the individual risks of death (adjusted HR 1.33, 95% CI 0.69-2.56, p=0.39), Q-wave MI (adjusted HR 1.15, 95% CI 0.30-4.47, p=0.84) and TVR (adjusted HR 1.06, 95% CI 0.52-2.15, p=0.87). The incidence of definite/probable stent thrombosis was relatively low (0.5% vs.0.9%, p=0.17) throughout the follow-up period."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "The 3-year clinical outcomes of patients treated with second-generation DESs are comparable to those treated with first-generation DESs for CTO-PCI."
          }
        ]
      },
